  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several proposed mechanisms for dielectric breakdown in interconnect low‑k materials and compares how each model depends on electric field. Across the top and middle, simple cartoons show different physical pictures inside an oxide or low‑k layer between metal/Cu lines: a thermochemical process driven by field‑induced bond stretching and bond breaking; anode hole injection and hydrogen‑release models in which injected carriers create or fill traps; an impact‑damage model involving Poole–Frenkel transport and trap creation; and Cu‑related models where copper ions drift from a Cu line through a barrier into the dielectric.\n\nA main theme is that breakdown is linked to defect generation, trap buildup, and sometimes hydrogen motion or Cu migration. The sketches distinguish thick versus thin oxides for hole injection, and show conductive carriers, trapped charges, and local damage sites forming near one electrode or within the dielectric.\n\nThe bottom panel condenses these ideas into a table, listing for each model which ingredients are included—carrier injection/conduction, ionization, hydrogen, bond breakage, Cu diffusion, trapping, and charge accumulation—along with the expected field dependence and a characteristic lifetime equation for time to breakdown. Overall, the figure is a comparative roadmap of competing breakdown models rather than a single experiment.",
